why 2 1/4 SIMILAR_MOTION All voices step down into V⁷ of E, the bass by a fourth. The descending line heads for the half cadence on E.
why 3 1/2 LEADING_TONE A deceptive move to F♯ minor (vi): the soprano takes A, so the alto's G♯ drops to C♯, the chord's fifth.
why 3 3/4 DOUBLING Soprano and bass share G♯ on a weak beat. The bass rises to A at once, and the soprano's G♯ belongs to the tune's decorated line.
why 3 7/8 LEADING_TONE The tune decorates its G♯ with F♯ before returning to G♯; Bach keeps the hymn melody. The music is heading for E, where G♯ is not a leading tone.
why 6 0 DOUBLING Soprano and tenor share G♯ in the E chord. The music is in E here, where G♯ is the third, not a leading tone.
why 7 1/2 LEADING_TONE The bass leaps G♯–C♯ to put the tonic in first inversion; the A that G♯ leads to is in the soprano at the same moment.
why 7 7/8 DOUBLING Alto (E–D♯–C♯) and tenor (B–D♯–E) pass through D♯ at the same moment from opposite directions. D♯ is a passing note in both, not a chordal seventh.
why 8 0 CROSSING The alto falls to C♯ while the tenor rises to E, so they trade places for a beat; on the next beat they are in order again.
why 9 1/4 DOUBLING Soprano (D–C♯) and tenor (B–C♯) meet on C♯ by step from opposite sides.
why 10 1/4 DOUBLING Soprano (B–C♯) and tenor (D–C♯) meet on C♯ by step from opposite sides.
why 15 0 DOUBLING Soprano (D–C♯) and tenor (B–C♯) meet on C♯ by step, as in bar 9.
why 3 1/2 OVERLAP The alto drops a fifth to C♯, below where the tenor just was, while the tenor steps down D♯–C♯. They meet in unison.
